;;; A string written in a guard never equals a value — M1e2 **W18**, register
;;; row `W18/str-in-guard` (`corpus/wrong_answers.toml`), found by S1e2.2.18
;;; (2026-11-04) probing the case's mechanism.
;;;
;;; ── What this program is ───────────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; Two `holds` facts, the atom `a` and the string `"b"`, which the loader
;;; holds as the symbol `b`. `g` keeps the `holds` value that equals `"b"`,
;;; and `n` every one that does not.
;;;
;;; ── The answer, by hand ────────────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; A string in a rule means what it means in a fact, so `(eq ?x "b")` holds
;;; for `?x = b` and `(neq ?x "b")` for `?x = a`: root derives `(hit b)` and
;;; `(other a)`. The blind enumerator proposes `(hit a)` and `(other b)`, so
;;; R is four states, root's holding the two. It is the answer of this file
;;; with `"b"` written `b` in its rules.
;;;
;;; ── What the engine answers ────────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; `g` never fires and `n` fires on both: the rule compiler lowers a guard's
;;; string to a raw node, which equals no value (`compile.rs`' `guard_arg`,
;;; `match_.rs`' `resolved_eq`), so `eq` never holds and `neq` always does.
;;; Root derives `(other a)` and `(other b)` and no `hit`; the candidates are
;;; `(hit a)` and `(hit b)`, and `ein solve FILE --minimal` at `ff023413`
;;; prints root's state as `(model)`, over `hit` alone, in a release build
;;; and a debug one.

(relation holds Node) (relation hit Node) (relation other Node)
(holds a) (holds "b")
(rule g () :match (and (holds ?x) (eq ?x "b")) :assert (hit ?x))
(rule n () :match (and (holds ?x) (neq ?x "b")) :assert (other ?x))
